{1 Installation}

Install the system dependencies: the PostgreSQL server, NPM, and a SASS
compiler ([sassc] or [sass]). On Debian-based systems:
{v
apt install postgresql npm sassc
v}

Install Ocsigen Start and all its OCaml dependencies using OPAM:
{v
opam install ocsigen-start
v}

Create your application using Eliom distillery:
{v
eliom-distillery -name myapp -template os
v}

Then, from the new [myapp] directory, create a local database (it runs
as your own user, with its files in [local_db/]), and compile and run
the application:
{v
make db-init
make db-create
make db-update
make test.byte
v}

The application is now available at
{{:http://localhost:8080}[http://localhost:8080]}. To create your first
user, sign up: if no mail server is configured on your machine, the
activation link is printed in the terminal.

The database server listens on port 3000. If this port is taken, change
[DB_PORT] in [Makefile.options] and [PGPORT] in [dune.config] before
running [make db-init].

The [README.md] file of the generated project describes the other
[make] targets and how to generate the mobile applications.

{1 I installed it and compiled it, what next?}

It is now time to learn Ocsigen! The template is a great way to get
started, because it comes with multiple runnable examples that show
you:

{ul
{li How to do remote procedure calls (RPCs);}
{li How to save session data (in {e Eliom references});}
{li How to use the notification system;}
{li How to create reactive pages;}
{li Many examples of widgets from {{!/ocsigen-toolkit/page-index}Ocsigen Toolkit}:
  {ul
  {li Carousel}
  {li Pages with several tabs}
  {li Time and date pickers}
  {li ...}}}}

Open the demo pages from the menu of the application. Their source
code is in the [lib/demo_*.eliom] files of your project. These files
are well-commented, so you can quickly find out how things work.

